var GTLearn = {
 "C1": "## Why it's here\n\nEverything up to now shows what you know and how you'd judge a moment. The practicum shows whether you can actually coach the lil' bird way with a real person while a trainer watches. It's the closest thing to the work itself.\n\n## How it works\n\n1. **Find a volunteer client.** Not a fellow trainee, and ideally not a close friend. Someone with something real but manageable to bring.\n2. **Get written consent** to the session being observed or recorded, and make clear it's a practice session within your training.\n3. **Run two full sessions** of 45 to 90 minutes, using the session architecture from R1. They can be Intensive sessions or standalone coaching.\n4. **A trainer observes**, live or by recording, and scores six behaviours from 1 to 4, plus guardrails if a guardrail moment comes up.\n\n## What the trainer is looking for\n\n- **Opening and contracting.** Relational capital built and a topic agreed by minute 10.\n- **Questions.** Mostly open, with \"Did I get that right?\" as a rhythm.\n- **Listening underneath.** Naming what might be going on, tentatively, in the client's words.\n- **Advice discipline.** Advice kept in your pocket, or offered only when asked, with your hat clearly off.\n- **Encouragement and honesty.** Encouragement that's specific and real, and hard questions asked again when they're needed.\n- **Action step.** The client's own: specific, with a day, worked on for the last 10 to 15 minutes.\n- **Guardrails** (if one came up). You paused, named it kindly, and followed the script.\n\n## Passing\n\nAn average of 3 or more, with no 1s, and a 4 on guardrails if a guardrail moment came up. You'll see your scores and the trainer's notes on this plate. If a session doesn't pass, you can book another one. That's what practice is for.\n\n!note You can't give what you don't have. By the time you get here, you've lived every session yourself. Trust that, coach the person in front of you, and let the framework sit in the background.",
 "C2": "## The final assessment\n\nForty questions drawn from every module's pool, mostly scenarios. Pass at 85%, with no harmful picks. Every crisis question must be answered at the lil' bird standard. It unlocks once every module check is passed.\n\nYou'll see every answer explained afterwards. If you don't pass, re-read the plates where you missed questions, then try again. You'll get a fresh set where the pools allow.\n\n## Sign-off\n\nOnce you've passed and every other gate is met, a trainer books a sign-off conversation with you. It isn't another test. It's a chance to look back over the course together:\n\n- What was your own two-degree shift from living the Intensive?\n- Where are you strongest as a coach, and where are you still growing?\n- How will you keep doing your own work after this?\n\nThen the **lil' bird Accredited** badge goes on your coachesandguides.com profile. In time, your profile will also be reachable directly from lilbird.life.\n\n## After accreditation\n\nAccreditation is a beginning. Keep getting coached yourself, keep practising, and come back to graduate Practice Circles when they run. Going up the mountain first isn't a one-time climb.",
 "F1": "## Why it's here\n\nEvery tool in this course sits on a handful of convictions. If you only learn the tools, you'll run the sessions. If you take on the convictions, you'll coach the lil' bird way even when a session goes nowhere near the plan. Start here, and come back here whenever you feel lost in a session.\n\n## My bias is you\n\nWhen anyone comes into contact with lil' bird, whether they're a client, in a group or an alum, the only agenda in the room is their highest good. Not our reputation, our curriculum, or a result we can put on a testimonial. Their good.\n\nIn practice it means two things. First, we believe the best about people. Second, we believe *they have the answer within them*, or they know how to find it. Our job is to help them get to it, not to hand it over.\n\n## The whole person\n\nThrough the research behind Roots & Wings, two things stood out. Very few people ever invest in their own self-awareness. And most people don't really know what coaching is or whether they're ready for it.\n\nWe also saw that we can't coach one box of a life in isolation. \"This is just a work thing\" is rarely just a work thing. Work, relationships, rhythms, faith and identity are all intertwined. So we go through someone's story, their wiring and all the areas of their life, because that's where the real thread usually is.\n\n## Identity is central\n\nMany coaching programmes do a personality test at intake and never mention it again. For us, who someone believes they are is core. It's where communication gets established, where trust gets built, and where transformation comes from. We don't just ask \"How do I solve this problem?\" We ask \"Where is this problem coming from?\"\n\n## The two-degree shift\n\nMost people aren't wildly off course. They've drifted a couple of degrees, and over years that takes them a long way from where they want to be. So we don't aim for a dramatic overhaul. We look for a small correction they can actually hold, then another. Change rarely happens in a moment. It happens in small steps, inside an environment and relationships that hold you to them. One day you look back and realise you've changed.\n\n## You can't give what you don't have\n\nYou can't take people somewhere you haven't been yourself. That doesn't mean you've lived their exact life. It means you've done the work of self-awareness and growth, and you keep doing it. A guide goes up the mountain first, so they know where the crevices are.\n\nThat's why you go through the Intensive yourself in this course. It's also why every coach on coachesandguides.com with a lil' bird badge is someone we know. They're not drowning, and they can hold space without getting triggered or caught in their own stuff.\n\n## What it sounds like\n\n- No overpromising. We never say \"do this for 30 days and you'll make 30k a month.\" That feels grifty, and change doesn't work that way.\n- Warm, a bit playful, then deep. A little whimsy helps people reconnect with who they were before life knocked them around. *Who were you on the monkey bars at eight?*\n- Natural, earthy language: seasons, gardening, pruning, rocks in the backpack, climbing mountains. Not coaching jargon.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Having an agenda.** The moment you need a session to go a certain way for your own sake, it stops being about them.\n- **Fixing.** Handing over an answer, however good, takes away the confidence they'd have built finding it.\n- **Coaching from an empty tank.** If you're not doing your own work, it shows. Usually it shows as impatience, advice, or getting hooked by what the client brings.\n\n!note A week of therapy brought back the memory of me at two, walking off down the road to visit Dad. That's when I saw how stuck I'd become. I was a leader and a coach, helping other people get unstuck, and a big part of me felt lost. We fill our backpacks with rocks when all we really need is to pack our favourite things and keep moving. That's the heart of lil' bird, and it's why you go first.",
 "F2": "## Why it's here\n\nA lot of coach training teaches you to be a passive mirror. You reflect back and stay out of the way. We go a step further. A lil' bird coach is an **active signpost**: very engaged, noticing things and pointing them out along the way, while keeping their advice in their pocket. Active without being directive. Guiding without putting a leash on someone and dragging them somewhere.\n\n## Before you start: clear yourself\n\nBefore a session, a meeting or even a phone call, I run three questions on myself:\n\n- Is there anything I'm trying to **prove**?\n- Is there anything I'm worried about **losing**?\n- Is there anything I'm trying to **hide**?\n\nThey strip away whatever falseness or fear I'd otherwise walk in with, so I come in as clean as I can.\n\n## Open, then clarify\n\nAsk open questions as much as you possibly can. The client should be the one exercising their muscles: thinking, sorting and coming up with things. A yes/no question asks almost nothing of them.\n\nThen follow up with a clarifying question: *\"So you said this, this and this. Did I get that right?\"* Open question, then a check. It's like playing tennis, hitting the ball back so they keep responding. Use their words, not yours. That rhythm makes sure you hear what they mean, not just what they say.\n\nThe follow-up I use most is simply *\"Tell me a bit more about that.\"*\n\n## Follow the heat\n\nWatch their body language, their eyes and their tone. Excitement and passion are hard to fake. When someone lights up, or their voice catches, follow it. That's usually where the real conversation is.\n\n## Silence is a tool\n\nLet silence run longer than feels comfortable. Your discomfort isn't necessarily theirs. You asked the question, and they're the one building an answer. If it goes too long, they'll usually ask you to repeat it, or lean in themselves.\n\nKnow your client. Internal processors may need less put on them in the moment and more homework or check-ins between sessions. External processors think by talking.\n\n## Run the session, gently\n\nWhen someone has talked for three minutes or so, it's okay to interrupt. I raise a hand so they can see I have a question, then play back what I heard in their words: *\"Hey, can I make sure I've got this? You said…\"* It turns a ramble into something they can confirm or correct. You're not telling them what to do. You're keeping the session on track.\n\n## Hearing underneath\n\nRepeating words back is a good start. The next step takes a little risk: *\"You've said these things. What I'm hearing, and correct me if I'm wrong, is that this might be going on.\"* Think of a basketball coach watching a shot: *\"Your arm's dropping. Did you notice? When did that start?\"* Point it out, then let them make the adjustment.\n\n## When you're not convinced\n\nIf something sounds wishy-washy, ask the question again, sometimes three or four times. I'm not telling them they're wrong. I want to be convinced that they believe what they're saying. *\"You said that. Let me ask it another way…\"*\n\n## \"I don't know\" and vague answers\n\n\"I don't know\" often means the question wasn't quite right. Ask: *\"Is it the question I asked, or do you genuinely not know?\"* Then simplify, reframe, or offer a binary to get moving. For vague answers (\"I just want more balance\"), ask *\"Tell me what balance means to you,\"* and keep gently taking layers off.\n\n## Advice stays in your pocket\n\nI rarely give advice unless it's asked for pointedly. Even then I say something like *\"I'm not the expert in your life. If I take my coach's hat off for a moment…\"* and keep it brief. The best thing I can offer is space for them to find their own answer, and the confidence that comes with it.\n\n## Humour, carefully\n\nHumour builds rapport and takes the brakes off. It can help people laugh at the situation, never at themselves. Read the room. When someone is vulnerable or in the middle of working something out, humour becomes a distraction. No sarcasm, no puns, no banter.\n\n!note I've always responded well to encouragement. Often I just need someone to say, \"I believe in you.\" I've also had coaches who were forthright with me and helped me face hard truths. A good coach knows when to stop, when to slow down and when to circle back. Their attention is on you, not on what they want to say next.",
 "F3": "> **Before launch:** the crisis and safety guidance on this plate must be reviewed by a qualified mental-health professional, and checked against US and Australian law. The phone numbers were current when written. Check them again before every cohort.\n\n## Why it's here\n\nCoaching and therapy overlap, and that's okay. Therapists use coaching postures, helping people decide what they want and move toward it. Coaches will sometimes go back into stories, emotions and old experiences. The overlap isn't the danger. The danger is a coach drifting into **fix-it mode with trauma or deep hurt**, or carrying on coaching through a crisis. This plate gives you the words for every edge you'll meet.\n\n## Name the edge early\n\nAt the start of an engagement, and again before the Story session, say it plainly: *\"Share as much as you're comfortable with. Just so you know, I'm not a trained therapist. This is coaching.\"* Your coaching agreement should also spell out the limits of confidentiality: if someone is at risk of serious harm, you may need to involve others. Saying it up front is far easier than finding the edge halfway through a session.\n\n## When a session goes deep\n\nIf someone starts unpacking a lot of hurt, don't dig for causes. Pause and honour it: *\"Thank you so much for sharing that. This clearly matters.\"* Then turn gently: *\"With an understanding of where you're at right now, how do you want to move forward?\"*\n\nIf it keeps pulling backward, session after session, ask yourself honestly: *Is this coachable, or does it need some inner work first, or alongside?* Then say it kindly: *\"I think some of what you're carrying would be really well served by a therapist, alongside what we're doing here.\"* Suggesting therapy isn't ending coaching. Often the two work well together.\n\n## The crisis script\n\nUse this whenever someone talks about suicide, self-harm, being harmed, or harming someone else. Stop the coaching agenda. It doesn't come back this session.\n\n1. **Stay calm and present.** Slow down. Thank them for telling you.\n2. **Ask directly.** *\"Are you thinking about ending your life?\"* or *\"Are you thinking about hurting yourself?\"* Asking directly doesn't put the idea in someone's head. It tells them they can be honest with you.\n3. **Check for immediate danger.** Do they have a plan, the means, a time? Are they safe right now?\n4. **If they're in immediate danger:** call emergency services (**911** in the US, **000** in Australia), or help them call. Stay with them, in person or on the line, until help is connected.\n5. **If it's not immediate:** share crisis support and help them use it.\n   - US: **988** Suicide & Crisis Lifeline (call or text 988)\n   - Australia: **Lifeline 13 11 14**\n   - Ask who they can be with or call today, and whether they have a doctor or therapist they can contact.\n   - Agree on one concrete next step before you finish.\n6. **If someone is being harmed or isn't safe at home:** ask whether they're safe right now. Call emergency services if they're in danger. Share domestic and family violence support:\n   - US: **National Domestic Violence Hotline 1-800-799-7233** (or text START to 88788)\n   - Australia: **1800RESPECT (1800 737 732)**\n7. **Don't promise secrecy.** You care about their safety more than about keeping a confidence.\n8. **After the session:** write down what was said and what you did, factually. Tell your lead trainer or supervisor the same day. Then look after yourself too.\n\nlil' bird coaches don't coach minors. If you learn that a child is at risk, contact emergency services or your local child protection authority.\n\n## Scope: medical, legal, financial\n\nWe don't give medical, legal, financial or benefits advice. If someone wants to stop medication, is weighing a legal action, or is deciding about money, encourage them to talk with a doctor, lawyer or financial adviser. You can still coach the *person*: how they want to approach the decision, what matters to them, who they'll ask. You never coach the technical answer.\n\n## Faith, and people hurt by faith\n\nLet people bring as much or as little of their faith as they want. Don't impose your own beliefs, and don't argue with theirs. For someone who has been hurt in a church or ministry setting, be gentle. Ask how they're doing after it all, and how much they want to talk about it. Reassure them they only need to share what helps. Coaching can be a safe place where they get to write their own story. Where there's trauma, therapy may need to come first or alongside.\n\n## Off-topic or no fit\n\nIf a client keeps raising topics that don't serve the work (politics, causes, other people's lives), redirect warmly: *\"That's really interesting. For our time, I want to keep us on what will help you most. Where would you like to go?\"* And if someone is consistently unwilling to engage, it's okay to name it, and to end the coaching kindly.",
 "I1": "## Why it's here\n\nBefore you go anywhere deep, you need two things: a picture of where this person is right now, and a sense of how they're wired. Life Canvas gives you the first. Inner Compass gives you the second. Just as important, this first session builds the relational capital you'll draw on for the rest of the Intensive.\n\n## Relational capital first\n\nA session won't work until the client feels it: *this person is ready for me, their attention is on me, I feel seen and heard.* So the opening minutes aren't small talk to get through. They're how you earn the right to go places later. Keep it brief, because the time is precious. But don't skip it.\n\n## Life Canvas\n\nLife Canvas is a stock-take of how someone is doing across the areas of their life: people, place, purpose, provision, health and more. It's loosely inspired by the Peace Index. Its real value is that people come back to it. It keeps their history, so you can both see trends: what's growing, and what hasn't moved. Those patterns often show what the coaching needs to focus on.\n\nHow I debrief it, using questions only:\n\n1. *\"Looking at this, what stands out to you?\"* Let them go first. Don't lead with your read.\n2. *\"What surprised you?\"*\n3. Go to the low scores *and* the high ones. *\"What's making this one strong?\"* tells you what they already know how to do.\n4. *\"If one of these shifted just a couple of degrees, which would make the biggest difference?\"*\n\n## Inner Compass\n\nInner Compass is lil' bird's profile. It draws on parts of the Enneagram, MBTI, attachment theory and the Big Five. But it's more than wiring: it also asks how someone is doing. That gives it a coaching edge.\n\nThe caution I always give: **none of this is about putting yourself in a box.** People grow and change. How self-aware they are, and how life is going, affects how they answer. A profile is a starting point for curiosity, not a verdict.\n\nHow I debrief it:\n\n- *\"What rings true?\"* and *\"What doesn't?\"* Both answers are useful.\n- *\"Where have you seen that show up this week?\"* Make it concrete.\n- *\"What would the people closest to you say about this?\"*\n\n## Why it matters for you as the coach\n\nUnderstanding someone's wiring changes how you coach them. An internal processor may need less put on them in the session and more to take away afterwards. An external processor thinks out loud and needs room to talk. Someone who feeds off encouragement needs to hear you believe in them. Someone who wants it straight needs you not to pull punches.\n\n## The action step\n\nEven an opening session ends with a step. Often it's small: noticing one pattern from the profile during the week, or a two-degree shift in the lowest Life Canvas area.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Reading the profile *to* them.** You become the expert on their life. Let them interpret it.\n- **Boxing them in.** \"You're a such-and-such, so you'll struggle with…\" closes doors.\n- **Rushing past the high scores.** Strengths are information too.\n- **Skipping relational capital** to \"get to the good stuff.\" You'll pay for it later.\n\n!note I do a lot of helping people understand how they operate. It helps them, and it helps me too. It tells me how to adjust how I communicate, and how I run the sessions, for each person.",
 "I2": "## Why it's here\n\nIdentity is built out of story, so we start there. Story comes early because this is where trust gets built. People often say things here they've never said to anyone. Some closure tends to happen in this session. So do tears. Be ready for both, and don't treat either as a problem.\n\n## How I run it\n\nA typical shape:\n\n1. **Open and contract (first 10 min).** Check in briefly, then say something like: *\"Share as much as you're comfortable with. Just so you know, I'm not a trained therapist. This is a coaching session, so keep that in mind.\"* Saying it at the start is far easier than finding the edge halfway through.\n\n2. **Walk the chapters.** Peaks, valleys, and the people who shaped them, for better and for worse. Ask, then follow up: *\"Tell me a bit more about that.\"* Every few minutes, play it back: *\"You said this, this and this. Did I get that right?\"*\n\n3. **The gratitude harvest.** Not a silver lining. Ask: *\"Looking back over all of it, who helped you get here, and what did they give you?\"* We're not saying the hard things were good. We're finding enough gratefulness to make peace and keep walking.\n\n4. **The action step (last 10 to 15 min).** It's theirs, it's specific, and it has a day. Often it's small: write to someone who encouraged them, or name one rock they're ready to take out of the backpack.\n\n## What to listen for\n\nThe heat: where their voice changes, where they speed up, slow down, or skip ahead. Names that keep coming back. Themes that repeat across chapters. Then risk the next step: *\"What I'm hearing, and correct me if I'm wrong, is that…\"*\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Digging for causes.** \"Why do you think your dad did that?\" moves you into a therapist's role. We notice. We don't excavate.\n\n- **Forcing the silver lining.** Gratitude that comes too fast is a way of avoiding things.\n\n- **Filling the silence.** Your discomfort isn't theirs. Let it go a little longer than feels natural.\n\n- **Letting the story eat the action step.** Watch the clock. The step is what you'll both come back to next session.\n\n## When to flex\n\nIf the story is still unfolding, split the session in two. Rushing it to stay on schedule costs more than an extra session. If trauma comes up, slow down. Thank them, honour what they shared, then turn gently: *\"With an understanding of where you're at right now, how do you want to move forward?\"* If it keeps pulling backward, suggest a therapist alongside coaching.\n\n!note My own story session took me back to being two years old. I set off down the road in my robe, with a backpack, a fire poker and a big bird toy, to visit Dad. That memory is where lil' bird came from. You won't know which memory will matter for a client. Make room for it to show up.",
 "I3": "## Why it's here\n\nStory looked back. Vision looks forward. People need a picture of their future that pulls them out of bed in the morning. Without it, even a clear next step feels pointless. But vision has a trap: fixate on it, and the gap between where you are and where you want to be gets so big it crushes you. So we name the vision, say it out loud, and *shift our posture toward it*, without making it a goal to hit by Friday.\n\n## How I run it\n\n1. **Open (first 10 min).** Check in, then pick up the action step from Story. Start with the human, then the commitment, so it doesn't feel like a report card.\n2. **Let them dream.** This is one of my go-to questions in almost every engagement: *\"If everything turned out as you imagined, the ideal, what would your life look like? What would you look back on near the end and say, 'I'm so glad I did that. I'm glad I took that risk'?\"* It can be as idealistic as it needs to be. We're not saying \"that's the goal.\" We're unlocking a part of them that feels free, and some confidence comes with it.\n3. **Find what's at stake.** The other side: *\"If nothing changed, what would that look like for you? Would you feel disappointed? Frustrated?\"* You could ask \"What's at stake?\" outright. I just tend to come at it a little more roundabout.\n4. **Find the driving thing.** Underneath the details: *\"What's the thing that, if it never happened, would really get to you?\"*\n5. **Shift posture.** *\"Knowing this is what you want, what would it look like to lean toward it this week, even a couple of degrees?\"*\n6. **The action step (last 10 to 15 min).** Theirs, specific, with a day.\n\n## What to listen for\n\n- **The heat.** Where they light up as they imagine. That's usually real.\n- **Shoulds.** A vision full of \"should\" is often someone else's vision. *\"Is that what you want, or what you feel you're supposed to want?\"*\n- **Fear running the show.** If every dream comes with \"but the money…\", don't circle the fear. Try *\"If money weren't the issue right now, what do you see?\"* Then reverse-engineer toward something real.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Turning the dream into a project plan** in the same session. Let it breathe first.\n- **Cutting the dream down to size.** \"That's not realistic\" from you kills what you're trying to unlock.\n- **Circling fear.** Talking about the fear for an hour never ends well. Acknowledge it, then come back to what they want *despite* the fear.\n- **Your vision, not theirs.** If you catch yourself getting excited about a future for them, check whose it is.\n\n## When to flex\n\nSome people find dreaming hard: high achievers, people in survival mode, people who were taught not to want things. Make it smaller and closer: *\"What would a really good ordinary Tuesday look like a year from now?\"* For an empty nester or someone between seasons, give permission to explore, the same way a kid heading to college does: *\"What's been percolating that you never had room for?\"*\n\n!note For years, when I thought about rest, ambition and success, I didn't know how to reach any of them. Later I realised it all flows from joy, from what I'm excited about and grateful for. I hold my expectations loosely now. If I'm enjoying it and people are being helped, the rest tends to fall into place.",
 "I4": "## Why it's here\n\nIdentity is one of my favourite sessions. It pulls together everything from Story and the opening profile and asks one question: *who are you?* Not who you're supposed to be, or who you became to survive. Who you are by default.\n\nAt lil' bird, identity is core, not an intake form. Who someone believes they are is where communication gets established, where trust gets built, and where real transformation comes from.\n\n## The kaleidoscope\n\nThink of identity as a kaleidoscope: nature, nurture, what energises you, what drains you. Turn it a little and a different pattern shows up. We aren't hunting for one neat label. We're following the threads.\n\n## How I run it\n\n1. **Open (first 10 min).** Check in, then pick up the last action step, human first.\n2. **Who you are by default.** *\"When you get up in the morning with nothing you have to do, what do you reach for? On a free Saturday, what brings you life?\"*\n3. **Before life knocked you around.** *\"Who were you at eight, on the monkey bars? In the sandpit at kindergarten? What did you care about back then?\"* A little whimsy helps here. It lets people reconnect with who they were before the hard lessons.\n4. **At your best.** *\"When are you at your best? What would the people closest to you say you're best at, or that you do without even noticing?\"*\n5. **What trips you up.** *\"What habits or traits do you notice playing against you?\"* Ask it with curiosity, not judgement.\n6. **Follow the threads.** Connect what you've heard to Story and to their Inner Compass, tentatively: *\"There's a thread here, and correct me if I'm wrong: you come alive when you get to make something from nothing. Does that fit?\"*\n7. **The action step (last 10 to 15 min).** Often it's about doing a bit more of what energises them, or a bit less of one thing that drains them, this week.\n\n## Going deeper: prove, lose, hide\n\nSometimes what's in the way is something someone is protecting. I sometimes ask clients the same three questions I ask myself before a session: *\"Is there anything you're trying to prove? Anything you're worried about losing? Anything you're trying to hide, maybe because you feel like an imposter, or ashamed of a past mistake?\"* Ask gently, and only when the relationship can hold it.\n\n## What to listen for\n\n- **Energy.** What they talk about fast and with their hands, versus what they describe flatly.\n- **Borrowed identity.** Titles and roles (\"I'm a manager, a mum, a fixer\") said as if that's all there is.\n- **What others see.** People often can't see their own gifts. What others say about them is gold.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Labelling.** \"So you're a creative\" closes a door. Keep it open: \"It sounds like making things matters a lot to you.\"\n- **Treating the profile as the answer.** Inner Compass is a starting point, not the destination.\n- **Staying in the past.** Childhood is a doorway to who they are now, not the place to stay.\n- **Rushing.** Identity often takes more than one session. I sometimes give it two, or two and a half. That's fine.\n\n## For the person who wants to be told what to do\n\nIdentity is where I start with someone who wants me to tell them the answer. *\"I could tell you what to do, but I'm not an expert in your life. I don't know your dreams, your wiring, or what keeps you up at night.\"* Make it a permissive space where there are no wrong answers. They have more in them than they think.\n\n!note I want lil' bird to be a place where people get in touch with that whimsical part of them, the person they were before life knocked them around and the hard lessons came. Learning to tap into how you're wired and what brings you joy and energy beats giving up and living on default.",
 "I5": "## Why it's here\n\nMost people who come to lil' bird are in the middle of a transition, or circling one: a career change, a move, a relationship ending or beginning, kids leaving, faith shifting. Transitions can feel huge, and sometimes they are. This session is about working out where someone actually is in theirs, and what they need to add to their tool belt to move through it with courage.\n\n## Clarify, explore or decide\n\nThe most useful thing you can do in a transition is to help someone see which of these they actually need:\n\n- **Clarify.** They don't yet know what's really going on or what they want.\n- **Explore.** They have a direction, but not enough information. They need to find things out or try something small.\n- **Decide.** They know enough. They need to choose and commit.\n\nPeople often try to *decide* when they actually need to *explore*. That's why they feel stuck.\n\n## How I run it\n\n1. **Open (first 10 min).** Check in, then pick up the last step.\n2. **Map the transitions.** *\"What transitions are you moving through right now?\"* There's often more than one.\n3. **Where are you in it?** *\"Do you know what your options are, or does it feel mostly unknown?\"*\n4. **The options question.** When options are on the table: *\"Is there one that feels like the easy way out? One you're a little afraid of? One you know deep down is the one you need to do, and you're just scared?\"* It helps people picture each path and see what they might be running from or hiding from.\n5. **What's underneath.** For a move or a big change: *\"Is this about wanting to explore and see what you're made of, or about needing to get away from where you are?\"* Then: *\"What would you be letting go of? What's unknown? How does that feel?\"*\n6. **The action step (last 10 to 15 minutes).** It should match where they are. If they need to explore, the step is a way to find something out. If they need to decide, it's a decision with a day.\n\n## Stuck between two options\n\nBe curious first: *\"Do you feel you need to pick one right now, or would you like to explore them?\"* If the stakes are small, encourage them to just try one. For bigger decisions, don't rush. Keep bringing them back to their motivations: *\"Why that one over this one? Have you tried anything like it before, and how did it go? Does it feel like something you want to do, or something you should do?\"*\n\n## Career transitions\n\nFor career changes, the step should **find information or options, not career suggestions from you.** Talk to someone who does the work. Read real job descriptions. Try something small. Never suggest jobs or titles they haven't raised themselves. If someone is under urgent money pressure (a job loss, income running out), coaching their job search isn't your role. Know where to refer them.\n\n## What to listen for\n\n- **\"Should\" versus \"want.\"**\n- **Fear wearing a sensible disguise.** \"It's just not the right time\" can be true, or it can be fear.\n- **Running versus going.** Moving toward something feels different from moving away from something.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Pushing a decision when they need to explore**, or letting someone explore forever when they really need to decide.\n- **Circling the fear** of money, of failing, or of what people will think.\n- **Picking an option for them.** Even subtly, through your enthusiasm.\n- **Making it bigger than it is.** Some transitions feel enormous but, in the bigger picture, there's less at stake than it seems. Gentle perspective helps.\n\n## The goal\n\nThe goal isn't just a good decision. It's helping someone trust their own decision-making: *\"I've thought it through as far as I can and let myself imagine the possibilities. I can trust this.\"*\n\n!note Leaving ministry didn't happen in one moment. There was a growing sense of a bigger calling: helping people become who they're designed to be, and the organisation I was in wasn't the best fit for my gifts any more. When I finally went out on my own, it felt very free and exciting. That freedom was on the other side of a lot of exploring first.",
 "I6": "## Why it's here\n\nVision and identity mean little if someone's week has no room for them. Life Rhythms looks at how someone's days actually go, compared with how they'd want them to go. It's practical, and it's often where the two-degree shift becomes most concrete.\n\n## How I run it\n\n1. **Open (first 10 min).** Check in, then pick up the last step.\n2. **The current week.** *\"Walk me through a typical week. What does it actually look like?\"* Ask for the real one, not the aspirational one.\n3. **The ideal week.** *\"Now, if your week were working for you, what would it look like?\"* Keep it grounded in who they are. Draw on what you learned in Identity about what energises and drains them.\n4. **The gap.** *\"What's not working? What do you need to cut out? What do you need to add?\"*\n5. **Check the essentials:**\n   - *Rest.* Is there enough of it, the real kind?\n   - *People.* Enough time with others, or enough time alone, depending on how they're wired?\n   - *Focus.* Time to zone in and do the work that matters?\n   - *Joy.* Are there hobbies on the board, or is it all work?\n6. **The action step (last 10 to 15 min).** Usually one small rhythm change for this week: something they'll stop, start or protect, with a day.\n\n## Scattered or rigid\n\nPeople tend to lean one of two ways. Some are **haphazard and everywhere**. They need a little structure and a few protected blocks. Others are **rigid**. Every minute is scheduled, and they need to throw some whimsy back into life to rediscover their joy. Notice which way someone leans before you suggest anything. Better still, let them notice it.\n\n## Small shifts beat overhauls\n\nWhen someone sees the gap between their current and ideal week, they often want to fix it all at once. Don't let them. A big overhaul rarely survives Wednesday. Ask instead: *\"What's the smallest change that would make next week feel noticeably better?\"* Removing ten things beats a plan to declutter your whole life.\n\n## What to listen for\n\n- **Obligation.** \"I have to\" and \"I should,\" over and over. Some obligation is healthy: caring for small kids is non-negotiable. But a life run entirely on obligation leaves no room for the person.\n- **Support.** Especially for new parents and caregivers: *\"What does the support around you look like?\"*\n- **The real cost.** High achievers will often describe an impossible week proudly. Don't strategise with them. Keep gently bringing them back to the cost: *\"What is this costing you, and the people you love?\"*\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Becoming a productivity coach.** Time-blocking tips miss the point. The question is what kind of life the week is building.\n- **Imposing your ideal week.** Your rhythms aren't theirs.\n- **Ignoring constraints.** A single parent's week has real limits. Work with them, not around them.\n- **Treating rest as a reward** for when everything else is done. It's part of the rhythm, not the prize at the end.\n\n## When to flex\n\nIf someone's week is in genuine crisis (caring for a dying parent, a newborn, a job loss), don't redesign it. Look for one thing that would help them get through, and support around them.",
 "I7": "## Why it's here\n\nNo one does life alone, and few things shape a life more than the people in it. This session takes an honest look at someone's key relationships: how they're doing, what would bring more health to them, and the relationships they're hoping for. Then it turns the mirror around. As we come to know ourselves better, how do we actually show up? What is it like to be on the other side of us?\n\n## How I run it\n\n1. **Open (first 10 min).** Check in, then pick up the last step.\n2. **The inventory.** *\"Who are the key people in your life right now?\"* Partner, family, friends, colleagues, community. Then, for each one that matters: *\"How is that relationship doing?\"* Keep it light. It's an inventory, not an interrogation.\n3. **Bringing health.** *\"For the ones that feel strained, what would bring a bit more health to them? Is there a boundary to set, a conversation to have, a move to make?\"*\n4. **Hoped-for relationships.** For someone hoping for a partner, deeper friendships or a community: *\"What are you looking for? What matters to you in the people you let close?\"* As they know themselves better, compatibility becomes clearer.\n5. **Turning the mirror.** *\"What do you think it's like to be on the other side of you? What would the people closest to you say?\"* This is often where the real growth is.\n6. **The action step (last 10 to 15 min).** Often a single conversation, a single boundary, or a single act of reaching out, with a day.\n\n## When someone blames others\n\nAt some point, almost everyone brings a boss, a partner or a parent who is the problem. Gently redirect: *\"There's nothing we can do to make other people change. All we can do is influence. What's yours to do here?\"* Help them move from looking at the other person to looking at themselves: *\"What part might you have played in how this got here?\"* That isn't letting the other person off the hook. It's putting the client back in charge of the only part they can change.\n\n## Boundary or barrier?\n\nLots of people come in wanting \"boundaries.\" Healing seasons and boundaries matter. But ask what they mean: *\"What does a boundary mean to you? Is this a boundary, or a barrier?\"* A boundary protects a relationship. A barrier ends connection. Both can be right. Knowing which one they're building matters.\n\n## Intentions versus experience\n\nEspecially with leaders: good intentions don't always land as good experiences for the people on the other side. A leader whose team doesn't trust them needs to see that. Ask where they see it: *\"Are people avoiding projects? Going quiet? Did something happen that broke trust?\"* Be incisive, and don't let them blame the team. A leader needs to be past that. Look at their own heart, tendencies and actions first, then at what might rebuild trust.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Becoming a couples counsellor** for an absent partner. You're coaching the person in the room.\n- **Taking sides.** You only hear one side, so stay curious, not convinced.\n- **Letting blame run the session.** Acknowledge it once, then turn toward what's theirs.\n- **Missing safety concerns.** If a \"difficult relationship\" sounds like control, fear or harm, go to the F3 crisis script. It's not a communication issue.\n\n!note People talk a lot about boundaries these days, and there's real wisdom in it. Some seasons really are for healing. I just like to ask what it means to them: is this a boundary, or a barrier? Getting honest about that is where things start to shift.",
 "I8": "## Why it's here\n\nAn Intensive can stir up a great deal: memories, dreams, new language for who you are, honest looks at relationships and rhythms. Without integration, most of it fades within weeks. This final session is the flight plan. It looks back over everything and names the ahas. Then it decides what to do about them, and what will keep the growth going once the Intensive ends.\n\n## How I run it\n\n1. **Open (first 10 min).** Check in, then pick up the last step.\n2. **Second Life Canvas.** Have them redo it before the session. Lay it beside the first: *\"What do you notice? What's moved? What hasn't?\"* Let them read it first.\n3. **The ahas.** *\"Looking back over our time together, Story, Vision, Identity, Transitions, Rhythms, Relationships, what were the moments that landed for you?\"* Let them say it out loud. They need to hear it in their own voice, not just yours.\n4. **What do you want to do about it?** For the two or three biggest ahas: *\"What would it look like to keep living that out?\"*\n5. **The supports.** Change happens in small steps held by an environment and relationships. *\"What are you putting in place so this keeps growing? Ongoing coaching? A group? A community? A rhythm of checking in with yourself?\"*\n6. **The final action step (last 10 to 15 min).** Specific, with a day, and connected to the supports they've chosen.\n\n## Making breakthroughs stick\n\nWhen a breakthrough happens, in this session or any other:\n\n- **Celebrate it.** *\"That's huge. That sounds worth celebrating. How would you like to mark it?\"* Celebrating helps cement it.\n- **Reverse-engineer it.** *\"How did that happen? Looking back, what did you do differently?\"* Saying it out loud is for their ears as much as yours.\n- **Turn it into a habit.** *\"You've done this once. If you want to do it ten times over the next month, what needs to be true for that to happen?\"* That's where accountability and a little strategy come in.\n\n## Being honest about the end\n\nNot everything will have shifted, and that's fine. Change rarely happens in a moment. Focusing on the dramatic shifts can even work against us. Celebrate the small changes that are real, and name the areas that haven't moved yet as places for future focus. There's no shame in it.\n\n## What to listen for\n\n- **Big promises.** \"I'm going to completely change everything\" often signals a crash coming. Bring it back to two degrees.\n- **Isolation.** Someone with no plan for support is likely to drift back. Gently explore what would help.\n- **Their words for the change.** The language they use to describe what's different is worth writing down and giving back to them.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Summarising for them.** Your recap of their ahas isn't the same as them naming them.\n- **Overselling the next thing.** If ongoing coaching would serve them, say so plainly, once, without pressure. It has to be their choice.\n- **Ending without a step**, because \"we're done.\" The final step matters most of all.\n\n!note A year later, honestly, the best thing is when a client doesn't say much at all, but I can see their life has changed. That means more to me than any testimonial. It's how I know what we do matters.",
 "R1": "## Why it's here\n\nYou've lived the Intensive and learned each session. This plate is the shape underneath every session, whether it's part of the Intensive or a one-off coaching call. Get the architecture right and the content can flex as much as it needs to.\n\n## The first 10 minutes: set the stage\n\nA session won't work until the client feels it: *this person is ready for me, I'm seen and heard, their attention is on me.* Build that relational capital first, but keep it brief. We hold this time as sacred.\n\n- **Check in.** *\"As you're coming in today, how are you feeling about things?\"* You don't need a detailed account of their week.\n- **Find the topic.** *\"What are you coming with today? Is there anything heavy you'd like to work on? A problem, or a solution you're after?\"* Get them thinking about what they actually want to talk about.\n- **Listen for uncertainty.** \"Well, maybe this, or that…\" tells you they haven't landed on a topic yet. Ask: *\"Tell me more about that.\"*\n- **By minute 10**, you're both confident about what the session is moving toward.\n\n## Checking in on the last step without a report card\n\nDon't start cold with \"Did you do it?\" Start with the human: *\"How's the week been? You seem excited. What's going on?\"* Then hand them the choice: *\"Where do we want to start? I do want to come back to how the action went. But if something else is on top, let's start there.\"*\n\nIf they didn't do it, watch for guilt or shame. Sometimes they realised it wasn't really what they wanted. That's partly on the coach, and partly just how sessions go. It's one reason coaching is a relationship over time, not a couple of sessions.\n\nBut if the same step has come back undone twice, it's time to be more incisive: *\"This is the second time we've landed here and it hasn't happened. Tell me more about why it's difficult. Is it that you don't actually care about it as much as you thought?\"* It's a provocative question, asked from care.\n\n## The middle: their time\n\nThis is the client's time to use the coach however they need. You don't need to know anything about the subject they bring. Your job is to create space and guide the conversation toward an action point.\n\n- **Audibles are normal.** If they bring something different from what you planned, even in an Intensive session with set content, go with it.\n- **Regulate the temperature.** Notice when the session runs hot (big feelings) or flat (defeated, lethargic), and adjust. Sometimes that means letting silence happen. Sometimes it means pushing in.\n- **Take notes**, especially their exact words, so you can give them back.\n\n## The last 10 to 15 minutes: the action step\n\nEvery session needs an action step that the client comes up with. Often it isn't the first thing that occurs to them. Leave proper time, because sometimes you need to go back into the cave to explore whether the step is right.\n\nA good step is:\n\n- **Theirs**, not yours.\n- **Specific**: what it will look like.\n- **Timed**: when they'll do it.\n- **Meaningful**: what they hope it will achieve.\n\nYou can't be accountable to anything you haven't decided to do. That's what you come back to next time, and that's what creates growth.\n\n## Homework: fit it to the person\n\nSome people love homework and set aside time for it. For them, the workbooks and exercises are gold. Others retain more in session, and homework is a single question to think about, or a reminder of their step. Internal processors often benefit from a reflective question to take away. Calibrate to the person, not the programme.\n\n!note Every session looks different. But at the end of the day, I want the client walking out with a clear step: when they'll do it, what it looks like, and what they hope it'll do. Sometimes getting there means going back into the cave. That's okay.",
 "R2": "## Why it's here\n\nEvery client is a kaleidoscope, and none of them fits a type. Still, some situations come up again and again. For each one below: what's often going on, a first move, a question to reach for, and the trap to avoid. Treat these as starting points for curiosity, never as scripts.\n\n### The high achiever quietly burning out\n\n**Often going on:** fixated on reaching something, at any cost, and often unaware of the cost. Extra tired, less motivated, relationships fraying, but driven by obligation to the goal.\n**First move:** don't put words in their mouth. High achievers usually can't be told anything. They have to see it, and when they do, they're often willing to change.\n**Reach for:** *\"What's at stake here? What patterns are you noticing?\"*\n**Trap:** sitting in strategy with them (\"try harder, smarter\"). Keep bringing them back to the real cost.\n\n### The mid-career person who wants to quit but is scared of the money\n\n**Often going on:** not enough vision yet to pull them through the fear.\n**First move:** find what's drawing them. *\"What in you is drawn to something else?\"*\n**Reach for:** *\"If money weren't the issue right now, what would you see happening?\"* Then reverse-engineer toward something real.\n**Trap:** circling the fear. That never ends well. Help them look at what they want, despite the fear.\n\n### The new parent who has lost their sense of self\n\n**Often going on:** years with no margin, especially for primary caregivers. Sometimes a career set down. Life driven by obligation, some of it healthy and necessary.\n**First move:** look at their support. *\"What does the support around you look like?\"*\n**Reach for:** *\"When did you first feel untethered? Was it a look, something someone said?\"* Exploring what they feel they've lost gives them a compass back, not to who they were, but to who they now have the chance to become.\n**Trap:** adding more \"shoulds\" to a life already run on them.\n\n### The person who wants you to just tell them what to do\n\n**Often going on:** often younger. They want to do it \"right,\" and don't yet trust their own thinking.\n**First move:** start with identity. *\"I could tell you what to do, but I'm not the expert in your life. I don't know your dreams, your personality, or what keeps you up at night.\"*\n**Reach for:** a little humour and permission. *\"There are no mistakes here. This might stretch your brain. That's normal. That's what coaching does.\"*\n**Trap:** giving them what they ask for. Telling them is never effective, unless they specifically ask for advice, and even then, sparingly.\n\n### The sceptic sent by a partner or company\n\n**Often going on:** they aren't sure coaching can help, or that they want it.\n**First move:** enjoy it. You'll have to earn them, and sceptics who come round often put the most in.\n**Reach for:** *\"What would make this worth your time?\"*\n**Trap:** ingratiating yourself or people-pleasing. That wastes time and isn't your job. If someone won't engage at all, it's okay to reach a natural ending.\n\n### The person who has recently left faith or ministry\n\n**Often going on:** more questions than ever, possibly hurt or burnout, and disorientation about what's next. A big opportunity for transitional coaching.\n**First move:** *\"How are you doing after it all? What stage of the process are you in?\"* Reassure them they can share as much as they're comfortable with.\n**Reach for:** *\"How much do you want to talk about your faith or your ministry experience?\"*\n**Trap:** going therapist where there's trauma. Ask honestly: is this coachable, or does it need inner work first or alongside?\n\n### The leader whose team doesn't trust them\n\n**Often going on:** a power dynamic, and often limited self-awareness. Best intentions that don't match what people actually experience.\n**First move:** if they've enlisted you, they usually know something's wrong. Help them see why it matters to be a leader worth following.\n**Reach for:** *\"Where do you see the lack of trust? What are people doing, or not doing?\"* Be incisive about their story.\n**Trap:** letting them blame the team. Keep putting it back on what they can own: their heart, tendencies and actions. Then strategy.\n\n### The person weighing a big geographic move\n\n**Often going on:** it feels enormous, and sometimes it is. But in the bigger picture there's often less at stake than it seems.\n**First move:** get curious about what's underneath. *\"Is this about exploring and seeing what you're made of, or about needing to get away?\"*\n**Reach for:** *\"What would you be letting go of? What's unknown? How does that feel?\"*\n**Trap:** deciding for them. The goal is that they come to trust their own process.\n\n### The empty nester asking \"what now?\"\n\n**Often going on:** one of the most abrupt changes of adult life. Twenty-odd years of being needed, then not.\n**First move:** *\"What's percolating? What are the losses, and the gains, you're feeling?\"*\n**Reach for:** permission to dream and explore, the same way a kid heading to college does. Exploring is possible even with a busy life.\n**Trap:** rushing them into a plan before they've let themselves wonder.\n\n### People you're not the right coach for\n\nChemistry matters. It's not everything, but it matters. That's why first calls are about chemistry, and why a diverse directory of coaches matters. Not everyone is ready, either. If someone consistently isn't putting the work in, it isn't worth their time or yours. It's fine to say so, kindly.\n\n## Adapting to the person\n\nYou'll shift how you speak with different people. Some want buttoned-up, some want warm and casual. Culture shapes us too. Some people take longer to get to the soft centre, while others open up quickly. Notice, adjust, and never assume from a category.\n\n!note A client was weighing up two or three options for a career shift. He worked in tech, so I said something like, \"I can understand it might get boring when you're paid $300k a year to make sure the same drop-down menu works day in and day out.\" Ridiculous, obviously. He laughed and said, \"That's so close to the truth.\" A little perspective, never at their expense, can open things right up.",
 "R3": "## Why it's here\n\nSome of the most lasting change I've seen happened in groups, and often I didn't do much. My job was to create the environment. In a group, people see themselves in each other, find courage in each other, and hold each other to the small steps. This plate is about holding a room so the group does the work.\n\n## The story behind it\n\nIn one coaching group, everyone did Life Canvas, and it turned out every person scored low on *place*: where they live and work. For some it was a cluttered home. For others it was an office so sterile nobody wanted to be in it. It was all taking up a lot of mental space.\n\nWe talked about the two-degree shift. Not \"declutter your whole house,\" but *\"What would it look like to remove ten items? Twenty?\"* It started small. One participant has since finished a second round of getting rid of a thousand items, and their life has opened up in ways nobody planned: clarity, focus, a great new job. Another spent a week setting up their office properly. When I spoke to them two years later, it looked even better. Something that had been a roadblock simply wasn't in the way any more.\n\nThat's the goal of coaching. And in that group, it happened mostly between them.\n\n## What changes in a group\n\nThe posture is the same as one-to-one: open questions, follow the heat, \"Did I get that right?\", advice in your pocket. What changes is that you're holding several people at once.\n\n- **Agreements first.** At the start: what's shared here stays here. Everyone shares as much as they're comfortable with. We listen more than we advise.\n- **Balance the airtime.** External processors will fill the space. Internal processors may say nothing unless you make room. *\"Let's hear from someone we haven't heard from yet.\"* Use rounds where everyone gets a turn.\n- **Turn the group toward each other.** Instead of you responding every time, invite them in: *\"What did you notice as you listened?\"* or *\"Where do you see yourself in what she said?\"* Ask for reflections, not advice.\n- **Name common threads.** *\"I'm hearing something similar from a few of you…\"* Shared themes are where group energy comes from, like *place* in that Life Canvas group.\n- **Short fishbowls.** You can coach one person for a few minutes while the others watch, then debrief. Keep it short, and only with willing volunteers.\n- **Everyone leaves with a step, said out loud.** Saying it to the group builds accountability. Next time, check in on the steps the same way as one-to-one: human first, no report card.\n\n## Regulating a room\n\nA group's temperature changes fast. One person's vulnerability can open the room, or overwhelm it. Watch for someone going deeper than the setting can hold. Honour what they've shared, gently bring the room back, and follow up privately. If anyone discloses something serious (self-harm, abuse, a crisis), pause the group and follow the F3 crisis script with that person privately, as soon as you can.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **Turning it into a series of one-to-ones** while everyone else waits.\n- **Letting the loudest voice set the agenda.**\n- **Advice ping-pong.** Group members \"fixing\" each other. Redirect to reflection: *\"What did that bring up for you?\"*\n- **Over-facilitating.** Talking too much yourself. Often the best thing you can do is ask a good question and get out of the way.\n\n!note In that group I didn't do much. I just created an environment where we could talk about what a small shift might look like. The group did the rest. Years later, those little steps had become something much bigger."
};
